    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having a real issue with some dark chocolate i'm working with. i'm seeing a lot of white bloom on the surface of the finished pieces and it's making them look really dull and unappealing. can you explain the science behind why this happens and what i should be doing with my tempering process to stop it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, white bloom on chocolate occurs due to the migration of cocoa butter or milk fat to the surface, which often happens when chocolate is not stored at proper temperatures. To prevent this, you should ensure your tempering process is precise: heat the chocolate to the right temperature, cool it down to a crystallization point, then reheat it slightly. Make sure the final melting point achieved is close to 88-90°F (31-32°C) for dark chocolate to ensure stable cocoa butter crystals. Store your candies in a cool, dry place and consider packaging to protect the surface from direct contact with air.",
      "source": "target",
